considering the patient had high-titer anti-GAD and a
relatively short course of disease. In addition, the high
risk of adverse effects and the uncertain clinical effi-
cacy of other immunotherapy (e.g., rituximab, cyclo-
phosphamide and mycophenolate) are considered.!*""!
Therefore, we decided to increase the dose of the
GABA-enhancing drug and add plasma exchange to
her therapeutic regimen, after which SPS symptoms
significantly improved and the memory decline partial-
ly recoverd. This case study indicates that anti-GAD
titer is not related to the severity of symptom, so titer
monitoring may be unnecessary during treatment."
So far, there has been no clinical consensus on the
treatment of this disease.
Anti-GAD antibody-associated AE combined with
SPS is extremely rare. Treatment with GABA-enhanc-
ing drugs and appropriate immunotherapy can improve</seg>
<seg id="s0004" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">the neurological function of the patients suffering from
the combination of SPS and LE. By reporting this case,
we hope to enrich the understanding of this kind of
disease.</seg>
